--TEST--
A matched route's declared name is the unit's own literal and its captures are the peer's, so only one of the two refuses a plain `string` — `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of the two things one `Core\Router\Match` carries: what
// decides the qualifier is where the value came from, and nothing about which
// member answered it. `name()` is the string the unit's own `#[Route(name: …)]`
// wrote, so a plain `string` target takes it and the first binding below
// compiles; a capture is a segment of the path the request arrived with, so the
// same target refuses it and the last one does not.
//
// Both halves are load-bearing. A match that marked the name as well would make
// a route's own name unusable at every sink it was declared for -- the metric
// label and the link builder -- and one that marked neither would launder the
// request path through the route table, which is the direction that costs.
var $matched = Core\Request::route();
if ($matched != null) {
    string $named = $matched->name() ?? "";
    foreach ($matched->params() as mixed $each) {
    }
    string $segment = $matched->param("id") ?? "0";
}
// covers: Core\Router\Match::name, Core\Router\Match::params, Core\Router\Match::param
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `string|uint|int|decimal|tainted string|Core\Uuid|Parses`
   --> case.nvs:18:23
